{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgjapjdy8jvawDjH9UN9i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0149F4775K85zEikyg1Acv6Z","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The viola part is the middle staff. At the start, before the first barline, there are 16th notes marked with abbreviation strokes. They alternate between low and high C, which is the C3/C4 figure. That stretch is bar 71, or at least its second half. The first bar of the figure lies at the end of the line before. The next bar starts with a low note and then short C4 notes, followed by a C4 half note with tremolo strokes. That is bar 72 (C3 8th, three C4 8ths, C4 half tremolo). The next bar has a half note with tremolo strokes, a quarter, then a rest or flag. That is bar 73 (C4 half tremolo, C4 quarter, rest quarter). Next comes a bar holding only a rest with a fermata above it. That is bar 74, the whole-bar rest. Then two slurred half notes marked \"pp\", falling a step. That is bar 75 (F4 half, E4 half). Then two half notes stepping down, marked \"poco cres\". That is bar 76 (D4, C4; the edition prints \"cresc. un poco\"). The last bar has a half note one step lower, which is B3. At the right edge there is an 8th rest and the start of the 8th notes, cut off. That is bar 77 (B3 half, rest 8th, C4 8th …); the line ends partway through it, and bar 78 begins the next line. Differences: the copyist abbreviates the 16th figures and tremolos with strokes, and the bar 74 rest carries a fermata. Neither changes the bar count.","right":true,"insights":"The middle section was easy to match: the fermata rest of bar 74, then the \"pp\" half notes and \"poco cres\" bars, fix the positions. The ends were harder. The copyist's stroke abbreviations hide the 16th and 8th rhythms of bars 71–72, and bar 77 is cut off at the right edge. A clearer scan of the line's first bar, or a word on how the copyist abbreviates repeated notes, would have settled whether the line starts mid-way through bar 71."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5451,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5451},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"419428ba-346d-41ea-9cfc-aa38d89df010","uuid":"a2a18f37-f8cc-41cc-9e2f-91d2dd3d1e44","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:58:44.955Z","request_id":"req_011CgjapjKceFkPCv1TMUjVE","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0149F4775K85zEikyg1Acv6Z":{"working":"The viola part is the middle staff. At the start, before the first barline, there are 16th notes marked with abbreviation strokes. They alternate between low and high C, which is the C3/C4 figure. That stretch is bar 71, or at least its second half. The first bar of the figure lies at the end of the line before. The next bar starts with a low note and then short C4 notes, followed by a C4 half note with tremolo strokes. That is bar 72 (C3 8th, three C4 8ths, C4 half tremolo). The next bar has a half note with tremolo strokes, a quarter, then a rest or flag. That is bar 73 (C4 half tremolo, C4 quarter, rest quarter). Next comes a bar holding only a rest with a fermata above it. That is bar 74, the whole-bar rest. Then two slurred half notes marked \"pp\", falling a step. That is bar 75 (F4 half, E4 half). Then two half notes stepping down, marked \"poco cres\". That is bar 76 (D4, C4; the edition prints \"cresc. un poco\"). The last bar has a half note one step lower, which is B3. At the right edge there is an 8th rest and the start of the 8th notes, cut off. That is bar 77 (B3 half, rest 8th, C4 8th …); the line ends partway through it, and bar 78 begins the next line. Differences: the copyist abbreviates the 16th 